Temperatures in the northern part of Nigeria can go as high as 48 ° C ( 118.4 ° F ) in cities like Maiduguri . The high temperatures coupled with an increase in insolation causes a region of low pressure to develop over west Africa and Nigeria ( between March to May ) . The Tropical continental airmass ( CT ) from the Sahara Desert in the northern part of West Africa , is weakened due to the overheating of the land surface in west Africa and Nigeria at this time . The Tropical continental airmass ( CT ) begins to retreat northwards to the Sahara Desert due to massive heating of the land which transfers heat in the form of convection into the Tropical continental airmass ( CT ) which constitutes the main layer of air above the land . This transfer of heat in the Tropical continental airmass ( CT ) in turn , causes the wind to expand and become lighter as this is the normal behaviour for winds moving above intensely heated grounds .","entities":["northern part of Nigeria","West Africa","Maiduguri","Sahara Desert","low pressure","wind","convection","Tropical Continental Airmass","Nigeria"],"facts":["West Africa -RELATED_TO-> temperature","Sahel climate -RELATED_TO-> northern part of Nigeria","low pressure region -LOCATED_IN-> West Africa","West Africa -RELATED_TO-> insolation","Intertropical Convergence Zone -RELATED_TO-> West Africa"]}
{"title":"Geography of Nigeria","text":"Geography of Nigeria
This climate , the tropical savanna climate exhibits a well marked rainy season and a dry season with a single peak known as the summer maximum due to its distance from the equator . Temperatures are above 18 ° C ( 64 ° F ) throughout the year . Abuja , Nigeria 's capital city found in central Nigeria , has a temperature range of 18.45 ° C ( 65.21 ° F ) to 36.9 ° C ( 98.4 ° F ) , and an annual rainfall of about 1,500 mm ( 59.1 in ) with a single rainfall maxima in September . The single Dry season experienced in this climate , the tropical savanna climate in central Nigeria beginning from December to march , is hot and dry with the Harmattan wind , a continental tropical ( CT ) airmass laden with dust from the Sahara Desert prevailing throughout this period .","entities":["Abuja","central Nigeria","tropical savanna climate","summer maximum","dry season","Harmattan wind","continental tropical airmass","Sahara Desert","rainy season","Nigeria"],"facts":["rainfall -RELATED_TO-> central Nigeria","tropical savanna climate -RELATED_TO-> Western Nigeria","Jos Plateau -LOCATED_IN-> central Nigeria","Monsoons -RELATED_TO-> central Nigeria","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> dry season"]}
{"title":"Geography of Nigeria","text":"Geography of Nigeria
Annual rainfall totals are lower compared to the southern and central part of Nigeria . The rainy season in the northern part of Nigeria last for only three to four months ( June -- September ) . The rest of the year is hot and dry with temperatures climbing as high as 40 ° C ( 104.0 ° F ) . Alpine climate or highland climate or mountain climate are found on highlands regions in Nigeria . Highlands with the alpine climate in Nigeria , are well over 1,520 metres ( 4,987 ft ) above sea level . Due to their location in the tropics , this elevation is high enough to reach the temperate climate line in the tropics thereby giving the highlands , mountains and the plateau regions standing above this height , a cool mountain climate . Seasons ( edit ) Nigeria , like the rest of West Africa and other tropical lands , has only two seasons . These are the dry season and the rainy season .","entities":["Southern part of Nigeria","dry season","northern part of Nigeria","Central part of Nigeria","Plateau regions","Alpine climate","rainy season","highlands","mountains","Nigeria"],"facts":["Niger delta area -LOCATED_IN-> Southern part of Nigeria","Bonny -LOCATED_IN-> Southern part of Nigeria","Warri -LOCATED_IN-> Southern part of Nigeria","Southern part of Nigeria -RELATED_TO-> Equatorial belt","Harmattan wind -RELATED_TO-> dry season"]}
{"title":"Geography of Nigeria","text":"Geography of Nigeria
The Tropical Maritime Airmass is a warm humid and unstable trade wind due to its warmth . Convectional currents are easily set up within the airmass whenever there is little instability in the airmass as a result of a slight to a very high orographic uplift in mountainous regions like the obudu plateau or the heating of the land which can trigger the formation of cumulonimbus cloud leading to thunderstorms within the airmass . During the dominance of the Tropical Maritime Airmass ( MT ) in the rainy season of Nigeria , mornings are bright and sunny , the sun 's heating of the land in the mornings and afternoons sets up convectional currents , these currents rise vertically and cumulonimbus clouds are formed , by afternoons to evenings , torrential downpour follows . The Easterly wave or the African easterly waves is another major contributor of rainfall during the summer monsoons months of May to September in Nigeria . The nature of this waves changes at about the 15 degrees line . The waves that pass south of this line carry moisture and create convection that leads to rain clouds .","entities":["rainfall","Tropical Maritime airmass","convectional currents","trade wind","Obudu Plateau","cumulonimbus cloud","thunderstorms","Easterly wave","summer monsoons","15 degrees line","moisture","rain clouds","torrential downpour","heating of the land","orographic uplift"],"facts":["rainfall -RELATED_TO-> central Nigeria","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> dry season","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> River Niger Benue trough","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> south western escarpment of the Jos Plateau","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> condensation"]}

