In these games , the fact that the enemies are being damaged is indicated by their behavior . On the other hand , many fighting games , such as the Street Fighter series , use easy - to - read health bars to clearly indicate the progress the player is making with each hit . It is common in first - person shooters to indicate low health of the player character by blood spatters or by a distorted red hue on the screen , attempting to mimic the effects of wounding and trauma . These visual effects fade as health regenerates . References ( edit ) ^ Jump up to : Moore , Michael ( 2011 - 03 - 23 ) . Basics of Game Design . CRC Press . pp. 151 , 194 . ISBN 1439867763 . Retrieved 2014 - 12 - 09 . Jump up ^ Chris Antista . `` The 10 most creative life bars '' . GamesRadar. 2010 - 08 - 17 . p. 2 . Archived from the original on 2014 - 12 - 28 .","entities":["Health (gaming)","first-person shooters","fighting games","Street Fighter series","health bars","Michael Moore","Basics of Game Design","CRC Press","Chris Antista","The 10 most creative life bars","GamesRadar"],"facts":["Health (gaming) -RELATED_TO-> health bar","Health (gaming) -RELATED_TO-> action role-playing games","Health (gaming) -RELATED_TO-> Hydlide series","Health (gaming) -RELATED_TO-> Ys series","Health (gaming) -RELATED_TO-> video games"]}
