Elm Park was Reading 's stadium for 102 years , pictured in 1981 In 1889 , Reading were unable to continue playing at Coley Park as WB Monck ( the local squire ) no longer allowed football due to `` rowdyism ( by ) the rougher elements '' . With club membership exceeding 300 by the time the club went professional in 1895 , Reading required a proper ground . A meeting the following year determined that funding would be difficult . £ 20 was donated by JC Fidler , on the proviso that `` no liquors were to be sold '' on site . The rest of the cost was financed through donations by wealthy supporters , as well as one large individual donation . A former gravel pit in West Reading was identified as the site . The first game at Elm Park was held on 5 September 1896 between Reading and A Roston Bourke 's XI . The visitors were a scratch team from Holloway College . £ 44 was taken on the gate , with an attendance of approximately 2,500 . In 1908 , the club 's annual general meeting proposed moving to a new ground near Reading railway station .","entities":["Coley Park","Elm Park","WB Monck","JC Fidler","West Reading","first game at Elm Park","A Roston Bourke's XI","Holloway College","Reading F.C. annual general meeting","Reading railway station","Reading F.C."],"facts":["Last competitive match at Elm Park -LOCATED_IN-> Elm Park","Reading match -LOCATED_IN-> Elm Park","last ever competitive match -LOCATED_IN-> Elm Park","Elm Park -LOCATED_IN-> Reading","Reading defeat Brentford 1–0 -LOCATED_IN-> Elm Park"]}
