So despite Hamilton 's best efforts to be remembered for certain things , history may see it a different way . Critical reception ( edit ) The Village Voice argues that this song 's refrain `` suggest ( s ) that we might look differently at the Founding Fathers and their ideas of freedom and equality , depending on who 's depicting them '' . NewYorkCityTheatre wrote that `` Eliza Hamilton sums up the story and leads the company to a moving finale , when the lights drop as the weight and vision of the American Dream continues its tug of war , between the immigrants who once founded this country and those looking to make her home '' . Variety wrote that `` in the end , Miranda 's impassioned narrative of one man 's story becomes the collective narrative of a nation , a nation built by immigrants who occasionally need to be reminded where they came from . '' Newsday described the finale as `` focused '' , while Uloop said it `` gives the heaviest meaning to any show in recent memory '' . The Huffington Post wrote `` The show ends with a bang , then a whimper , then a harmony . ''","entities":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story","Eliza Hamilton","Hamilton","Miranda","The Village Voice","NewYorkCityTheatre","Variety","Newsday","Uloop","The Huffington Post","Founding Fathers","freedom","equality","American Dream","immigrants"],"facts":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Cast of Hamilton","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> show tune","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> legacy","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> English","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Lin-Manuel Miranda"]}
